Sampling techniques are classified into two categories:

Probability Sampling
Non-Probability Sampling
Probability Sampling
(a) Simple Random Sampling

Every member of the population has an equal chance of being selected.

Example:

Selecting people randomly.
Drawing names randomly from a class of students.
(b) Systematic Sampling

Every nᵗʰ member of the population is selected after choosing a random starting point.

Examples:

Airport survey: Give a credit card form to every 5th, 10th, or 20th passenger
Feedback survey: Send a feedback email to every 10th customer.
(c) Stratified Sampling

The population is divided into strata (groups) based on similar characteristics, and random samples are selected from each group.

Examples:

Divide employees by department and randomly choose people from each department.
Divide people by age groups:
Below 12 years
12–18 years
Above 18 years
Political surveys.
(d) Cluster Sampling

The population is divided into clusters, and some clusters are selected randomly. Every member within the selected clusters is surveyed.

Example:Randomly selecting several schools from a district and surveying all teachers working in those schools.
(e) Multistage Sampling

A combination of different sampling methods.

Usually, clusters are selected first, followed by random sampling within those clusters.

Example:

Randomly selecting cities.
From each selected city, randomly selecting households for the survey.
Non-Probability Sampling

Individuals are selected based on convenience or the researcher's judgment.

(a) Convenience Sampling

Participants are selected because they are easy to reach.

Example:Surveying people at a shopping mall.
(b) Judgmental (Purposive) Sampling

The researcher selects participants based on personal judgment or specific characteristics.

Example:

Choosing experienced employees for a survey.
Selecting people with expertise in a particular field.
(c) Snowball Sampling

Existing participants recruit new participants from among their acquaintances.

Example:

Surveying people with a rare disease.
(d) Quota Sampling

Participants are selected until a fixed quota is reached for each category.

Categories may include:Age
Gender
Occupation
Caste
Income Group

Note: The choice of sampling technique depends on the research objective and use case.
